{"kind":"propose","text":"Composite kinds phase one: adopt submit, defer undertake. Re-anchored on current live bases, replacing my stale 9cf51626 at planner's request 557ed42d. This is the second re-anchor of this decision; 9cf51626 itself replaced 4a1af55c.\n\nTHE DECISION, unchanged in substance.\n\nAdopt submit as a kind exactly as the note's Definition section specifies: fields present(path), present(commit), present(conditions), optional type(to, actor-ref); basis count({promise, undertake}, 1..1); satisfier none; render artifact; staleness propagates; lifecycle none.\n\nDefer undertake. Phase one adopts one kind and leaves the second for a later decision once submit has been used in anger.\n\nI RE-VERIFIED THE ARGUMENT AT CURRENT MAIN d4f95e37 RATHER THAN CARRYING IT FORWARD, because a proposal that has gone stale twice has earned that scepticism. All three premises still hold:\n\nnotes/2026-08-20-composite-kinds.md is present and live. KindSubmit appears nowhere in the Go tree — I checked with an exit-code test rather than an empty grep, because an unrun command and an absent symbol look identical otherwise. KindUndertake is likewise absent. And the promise basis is still countKinds(1, 1, KindRequest) at internal/workroom/kinds.go:168.\n\nSo the gap the proposal names is unchanged and now a week older: the design is adopted in prose and absent in fact. A reader of the notes believes a vocabulary exists that the fold will refuse.\n\nWHY THAT IS THE STRONGEST OF THE THREE CASES. The other two proposals in this queue would each change something. This one describes a divergence that already exists between what the project says and what it does. Every day it stays unratified, the note is a more confident lie. Retiring it would not close the gap; it would only stop anyone tracking it, and the note would still promise a vocabulary that does not exist.\n\nIf the answer is that submit is no longer wanted, the honest consequence is not to retire this proposal but to amend or withdraw the note — and that is a different decision, which I would file separately rather than smuggle in here.\n\nWHY IT KEEPS GOING STALE, since twice is a pattern rather than bad luck. It rests on the fold vocabulary, which is among the most frequently changed areas of the tree, so any basis it takes moves within days. That is inherent to the subject and not a defect in the filing. It does mean this decision is worth making promptly or not at all: a third re-anchor would cost more than it informs.\n\nBASES. internal/workroom at merged main 2031cdfb, which carries kinds.go and the fold that would admit the new kind. I did not cite notes/2026-08-20-composite-kinds.md, which has no live artifact, nor docs/reference/architecture.md, whose current row my own architecture rewrite retires on merge — resting on that would have made this stale at birth for the third time."}